{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  "WHO ARE YOU LOOKING FOR?" — the front door to the search that already exists
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ── WHY THIS IS A BAND AND NOT A SEARCH PAGE ────────────────────────────────

  The obvious build for this design was a new `/search` with its own service. It would
  have been the same fault this repository has just spent a commit fixing on `/help` and
  `/support`: two front doors to one job, each unable to say the other exists.

  `ActivityFeedService` already searches announced results, categories, programmes,
  nominees, registry profiles, posts, events, discussions, cycle phases and the site's
  own pages. What it did NOT have was an entrance framed around the question people
  actually arrive with. It is called "Activity", it is filed under a heading about what
  is happening, and nobody who wants to find a principal types the word activity.

  So this is a door, not a second room. It submits to the same GET endpoint the search
  page's own form submits to, so the two can never disagree about what the site holds.

  ── THE CAPTION IS GENERATED, AND THAT IS THE POINT ─────────────────────────

  A sentence naming what a box searches is exactly the kind of claim this codebase has
  been burnt by: `/cookies` said in bold "We set one cookie" while three were being set,
  and every word of it had been true the day it was typed. So the nouns come from
  `ActivityFeedService::SOURCES` through `search_covers()`. Adding a source adds a noun;
  a source with no noun declared is covered by the closing clause rather than silently
  dropped from a promise.

  ── THE PROMISE UNDER IT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS ────────────────────────────

  "Nothing unannounced is searchable" is not decoration. Until this commit the search
  returned nominees crowned during `judging` labelled "Winner", with their category — an
  announcement made by a search box for an award nobody had announced. It is gated now,
  and `UnannouncedResultTest` is the sentence's evidence. Do not print this line on a
  surface that does not go through that gate.

  PARAMS
    heading      — override the question. Defaults to the design's own.
    placeholder  — override the field's hint.
    q            — the current query, echoed back so the field survives a submit.
    compact      — true on a page that already has a heading above it.
    live         — true to carry the `data-act-*` hooks the activity page's script
                   upgrades into an ARIA combobox. Opt-IN, because the attributes are
                   meaningless without that script and the script exists on one page.
    min_query    — when set, states the shortest query worth submitting.
#}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('/assets/css/components/find.css') }}">

<section class="fb{% if compact|default(false) %} fb--compact{% endif %}" aria-labelledby="fbHeading">
  <div class="fb__in">
    <h2 class="fb__q" id="fbHeading">{{ heading|default('Who are you looking for?') }}</h2>

    {# A real GET form, and the only one on this band. It posts to the search page
       rather than to an endpoint of its own: with scripting off, on a browser too old
       for fetch, or on a connection that dropped the bundle, this still works — which
       on this platform is routine rather than an edge case. #}
    {# `data-act-form` / `data-act-input` are the hooks the activity page's own script
       looks for. They ride on THIS form because this band replaced that page's separate
       field — one search surface, one input. The script bails out quietly when they are
       absent (`if (!form || !input) return;`), so dropping them would have cost the live
       as-you-type search with nothing failing anywhere. ActivityPageAccessibilityTest
       and FindBandTest both pin them for that reason. #}
    <form class="fb__form" method="get" action="/activity" role="search"
          {% if live|default(false) %}data-act-form{% endif %}>
      {# A real label, not a placeholder. A placeholder disappears the moment somebody
         types, is not reliably announced, and is the single most common way a search
         field becomes unusable for anybody who needs the name of the thing they are
         filling in. `.sr-only` — the class is NOT `ag-sr-only`, which does not exist
         and once pushed this site's Search button off the edge of a phone. #}
      <label class="sr-only" for="fbQ">{{ heading|default('Who are you looking for?') }}</label>

      <svg class="fb__mag" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
           stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"/><path d="m20 20-3.5-3.5"/>
      </svg>

      <input class="fb__input" id="fbQ" type="search" name="q" value="{{ q|default('') }}"
             autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
             placeholder="{{ placeholder|default('A name, an award, a category or a school') }}"
             {% if min_query|default(0) %}aria-describedby="fbNote"{% endif %}
             {% if live|default(false) %}data-act-input{% endif %}>

      {# Visible on its own row rather than tucked inside the pill. A submit button that
         is only an icon inside a rounded field is the pattern that fails a 24×24 target
         check and reads as decoration. #}
      <button class="fb__go" type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>

    <p class="fb__note" id="fbNote">
      {%- if min_query|default(0) %}
        <span class="fb__min">Type at least {{ min_query }} characters.</span>
      {%- endif %}
      {%- set _covers = search_covers() -%}
      Searches
      {%- for noun in _covers %}
        {{- ' ' }}{{ noun }}{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
        {%- if loop.last %}, and everything else published here.{% endif %}
      {%- endfor %}
      <span class="fb__promise">Nothing unannounced is searchable.</span>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
